in this video we'll talk about cell  lines and cell strain  cell line is the general term that  applies to different populations of cell  that can be maintained in culture in  vitro  this is a portion of the intestine so we  can culture these intestinal cells as a  cell line and we can call it an  intestinal cell line there are different  features of the cell light such as they  can be maintained for an extended period  of time it should be retaining stability  in terms of certain phenotypes and  function  cell line is generally clonal that means  from the parental cell line there could  be  clonal progeny  when it comes to feature of the cell  line cell lines are generated from  subculturing a primary culture  and the cell line usually display  functional similarities with the primary  cell with certain degree of  dissimilarities in terms of genotype and  phenotype there could be modifications  cell lines are divided into two groups  based on the growth patterns  so finite cell line divides for limited  number of times whereas the continuous  cell line divides  for infinite numbers of time  finite cell line  has  limited division capability that means  they would divide for 20 or less 100  times for a finite number and they would  not divide anymore they would eventually  die  and the time  or the number of times they divide  before dying is governed by several  factors such as lineage differences  species  culture conditions and media conditions  now let me tell you that finite cell  lines are generally adherent in nature  that means they would stick to the  surface of the  culture plate  now infinite cell line in contrast can  grow for indefinite number of times they  generally divide faster compared to a  finite cell line  now these cells can eventually grow on  top of one another and they are forming  a multi-layered structure like a tumor  so infinite cell lines are pretty much  like a tumor or a cancerous cell  now generally normal cells are obtained  from tissue or organ  and you form a primary culture out of  them primary culture is then subcultured  after subculture you generate cell line  from that cell line you can select a  portion a subset of that cell line and  you can make them immortal and make them  a immortalized cell line or also you can  take the cells from a cancer cancer  patient and make a cancer cell line  which are immortal  so moral of the story cell strain is one  subset of a cell line  and they are selected  based on specific characteristics  cell strain will often undergo  additional genetic changes compared to  the parent cell line  so moral of the story or the take home  message is the cell strain is one kind  of subset of a cell line  the first continuous cell line ever  established was the gila cell line taken  from henrietta lacks who was a carcinoma  patient cervical carcinoma patient and  this was the first aneuploid line  derived derived from human tissue  and so far it has it has been used for  several purposes throughout the world  now let me tell you the example of cell  strain hmc one cell line is a frequently  used cell line to study mass cells so  basically this is muscle leukemia cell  line  so whenever we want to study some  aspects of muscle we can use this cell  line  now there are two variants of this cell  line hmc 1.1 and hmc 1.2 both these cell  variants are actually the cell strain or  a subset that are selectively  selected differently based on specific  mutations  okay let's talk about the applications  of cell line in research  in  molecular biology or cell biology lab  cell cultures are kind of bread and  butter technique every day people are  doing cell culture  now let's say you have  figured out a particular novel protein  protein x let's say and wanted to  understand when this particular protein  is localizing inside the cell  so you can clone that particular protein  in an expression vector transfected into  the cell line and then ask the question  where this gfp bound protein is  localizing and you can get your answer  by doing some microscopy technique in  this case you can see the protein is  localized in the nucleus because the  fluorescence is coming from the nucleus  this is one type of question there are  many other questions that can be  answered in a research settings for  example you have discovered a drug which  might have a anti-cancerous property how  do you test that you can use some  cancerous cell line and in one set you  don't treat it with anything and in  another set you treat it with this  particular drug and then you check  viability for both these cases if you  see a decrease in viability that means  your drug might be very efficient people  also create efficacy curves from this  particular  assays  there are many applications of cell line  in industry let me tell you one of that  so whenever you need antibodies you  order from the companies and the  companies generate antibody using  hybridoma technology which is a way of  generating monoclonal antibodies in  hybridoma technology a hybrid of  antigen specific b cell is fused with  myeloma cells how it is in specific b  cells can generate all the monoclonal  antibodies but they are finite they are  mortal so they has to be fused with  myeloma cells to produce a hybridoma  this hybridoma can be used to generate  monoclonal antibodies generation after  generation and that is how these  industrial companies can supply you with  antibody years after years  now let's talk about talk about some  clinical applications of cell line  so in clinical diagnosis several cell  lines are used one such example is to  detect presence of antinuclear  antibodies in patient's blood sample for  example if a patient has systemic lupus  arithmeticist they might have particular  anti-nuclear antibody in their blood  sample that can be detected using this  hep 2 cell line f2 cell line has all the  native protein arrays  and hundreds of antigen  which provides an ideal substrate for  ana detection so patient sample might  have these auto antibodies might not  have them using this particular cell  line and a combination of  immunofluorescence we can understand  whether the patient sample has  anti-nuclear antibodies or not looking  at the patterns  there could be several clinical  interpretations that can be made from  these immunofluorescence data so that is  why usage of cell line is crucial in  medical diagnosis as well and we give  just one example  but cell lines are very different from a  native tissue let me tell you why let's  say you have a portion of the brain a  neural tissue that would have different  cell types they would have they would  have abcd neurons different type of glia  astrocytes all your render sites etc but  a neural cell line would have isolated  or clonal population of only neurons  that means they would create synapses  but the way they create synapse synaptic  strain properties might be very  different from a native or a primary  tissue  that is why when using cell line we have  to think about some limitations and this  is why people these days generate 3d  cultures which mimics more like organ  and these are known as organoids for  brain  cerebral organoids are generated for  many other tissues organoid are  generated if you are interested you can  click on the i button to know more  there are several limitations of the  cell line as we have mentioned and we  should know this because when we are  using cell line for our own usage we  have to understand the limitations first  of all serial passage of sub culturing  leads to acquisition of several  mutations over time  that can lead to genetic drift and that  can cause several heterogeneity in the  culture at a particular time point so  obviously cell lines has many  limitations  cell lines never recapitulate the  entire property of a primary culture it  recapitulate only certain aspects  immortalized cell lines might have  different chromosomal abnormalities  aberrations and mutations so they do not  permit  uh studying several biological process  and we have to be very careful when we  think and design our experiments using  these cell lines  so you can get notes and flash cards  regarding this topic and many other  topics in my facebook channel in my  facebook page so please share the link  of my facebook page with your friends  you would get many dynamic flash cards  which would help with your preparation  and understanding as usual don't forget  to like share and subscribe you can  support my channel on patreon super like  or if you're an indian viewer you can  support me via beam upi you can scan  this qr code to pay me  anyway  my courses are also present in academy  which is india's biggest online learning  platform you can use my code ap 10 to

